WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.735
-Our next guest is a
Tony Award-nominated actress

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.870
you know from shows like
"Only Murders in the Building"

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:05.705
and "The Idol."

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:07.039
She stars in "The Holdovers,"

00:00:07.039 --> 00:00:08.341
which is in theaters now.

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.375
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:12.311
-And neither of them
made it to 25.

00:00:12.311 --> 00:00:16.115
My baby wasn't even 20.

00:00:16.115 --> 00:00:18.451
-I'm so sorry.

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:20.753
-I took this job...

00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:23.056
when Curtis was small
because I wanted to ensure

00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:25.291
that he was going
to have a good education.

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:27.126
You know he flourished here?
-Yes.

00:00:27.126 --> 00:00:29.796
No, he was a great kid.
I had him one semester.

00:00:29.796 --> 00:00:31.430
Very insightful.
-Mm-hmm.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:34.266
He hated you.

00:00:34.266 --> 00:00:37.569
He said you were
a real ass[bleep].

00:00:37.569 --> 00:00:39.138
-Well, uh, like I said...

00:00:39.138 --> 00:00:40.572
[ Chuckles ]

00:00:40.572 --> 00:00:42.375
...sharp kid, insightful.

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:44.243
[ Laughter ]

00:00:44.243 --> 00:00:47.213
-Please welcome to the show
Da'Vine Joy Randolph, everybody.

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:50.216
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:50.216 --> 00:01:07.367 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:09.702
Welcome to the show, Da'Vine.
How are you?

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:14.106
-I'm good. I'm in New York
at the perfect time.

00:01:14.106 --> 00:01:15.241
-It is a good time to be here.

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:17.677
-The trees are up,
the lights are up.

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:19.312
-And you like this?
-Yes.

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:20.647
-Okay, good.
-To watch.

00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:21.981
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-To watch. Okay.

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:25.118
-Right, because it's now like,
okay, it's holidays now.

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:26.619
-Yeah.
I feel like they just put it up

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:28.587
like yesterday.
-Yeah. It just went up.

00:01:28.587 --> 00:01:30.857
I like it less because
I have to come here every day.

00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:32.791
-Yeah. You got to
fight through the crowds.

00:01:32.791 --> 00:01:34.360
-...and you're like,
"Oh, my God! Christmas!"

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.296
And then you get to leave and
don't have to worry about it.

00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:37.564
-Yeah.
-And every day I'm like,

00:01:37.564 --> 00:01:38.998
"Why are all these people
at my office?"

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:40.833
-Yes, yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:42.201
-This is a beautiful film.

00:01:42.201 --> 00:01:43.803
It's a wonderful holiday film
as well.

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:45.805
It's about Paul Giamatti
is a teacher

00:01:45.805 --> 00:01:47.306
who stays at the school
for kids

00:01:47.306 --> 00:01:48.641
who can't leave
for the holidays.

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:51.844
You are the woman
who works in the cafeteria,

00:01:51.844 --> 00:01:54.047
and it's basically
the three of you,

00:01:54.047 --> 00:01:55.013
you and Paul and a student.

00:01:55.013 --> 00:01:56.048
It's a beautiful film.

00:01:56.048 --> 00:01:57.883
It feels like it was made
in the '70s.

00:01:57.883 --> 00:01:58.951
-Yeah.
-There's all these

00:01:58.951 --> 00:02:00.220
lovely touches, but I imagine

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.354
while you were making the movie,

00:02:01.354 --> 00:02:02.688
you wouldn't know what it was

00:02:02.688 --> 00:02:04.156
going to look like
when it was done.

00:02:04.156 --> 00:02:07.960
-No, so Alexander Payne does
something very I think amazing.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.262
He doesn't use Video Village.

00:02:10.262 --> 00:02:14.467
Video Village is like
with these monitors you can see,

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:15.968
you know, like, what's
going on --

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:18.805
-In between takes, you can go
back and see how the last one

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:19.973
looked.
-None of it.

00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:23.242
And so he literally
is in the room filming

00:02:23.242 --> 00:02:25.111
and then he has his face
right --

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:28.281
so the camera's here
and his face is right here.

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:30.350
And he'll be like,
[whispers] "Action."

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.418
-Wow.
-It's -- You know what I mean?

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:34.354
Because, you know,
you're used to like, "Action!"

00:02:34.354 --> 00:02:35.988
-'Cause they're way far away.
-Yeah.

00:02:35.988 --> 00:02:38.124
So it's like
it's very intimate.

00:02:38.124 --> 00:02:41.493
It feels like this very
fly-on-the-wall type thing

00:02:41.493 --> 00:02:43.129
and we're just being.

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:46.899
And so we had no idea -- Like,
you know, our clothes were like,

00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:49.435
oh, okay, so our clothes
look vintage-y

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:51.671
and we're in these, like,
old abandoned buildings.

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:54.574
But you have no idea,
and then you see the movie

00:02:54.574 --> 00:02:56.609
and you're like, "Whoa."
-Yeah, you really feel

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:58.344
like this could have
been made in the '70s.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:02:59.746
-We really transported there.

00:02:59.746 --> 00:03:02.682
And I think also the city
of Boston is perfect

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:04.050
for that, too,
you know what I mean?

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.485
And like, the more you get
northern of like northeast,

00:03:06.485 --> 00:03:07.820
it's timeless.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.922
-So those are people
that are resistant to change.

00:03:09.922 --> 00:03:11.324
-Yes!
[ Laughter ]

00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:12.558
-They're like, "We got it right.

00:03:12.558 --> 00:03:14.360
We're not going to make it
a lot fancier."

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.996
-Yes.
-You want fancy go down south.

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:21.234
So another thing I heard is that
you working in the kitchen,

00:03:21.234 --> 00:03:23.369
you told Alexander Payne
that you wanted to actually

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:25.104
physically cook the food.
-Yeah.

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:28.107
So I really love to cook,
and I just,

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:30.810
I needed something
with my hands

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:32.845
and I knew that it would
ground me in the character.

00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:39.818
And so in the movie
there is a soup, oatmeal,

00:03:39.818 --> 00:03:42.755
like she's prepping and
chopping up all the vegetables,

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:46.125
a roasted chicken, and, like,
a big pot of scrambled eggs

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:48.661
as like the hangover meal
or whatever.

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:52.599
And I loved all of it.
And he was so kind.

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:57.169
He basically was like, "Okay,
what is the Christmas menu?"

00:03:57.169 --> 00:03:59.505
And so I get to pick out
what the Christmas menu

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:01.107
was and all the trimmings.

00:04:01.107 --> 00:04:04.510
And then they had --
There's a series called "Julia,"

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:05.645
like the Julia Child series.
-Yes, of course.

00:04:05.645 --> 00:04:07.046
-I think it was on HBO.

00:04:07.046 --> 00:04:10.683
And the people from there
worked with us,

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:13.052
and so I got to just like,
geek out on, like,

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:15.220
food nerdy stuff.
-Very nice.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:18.457
-Yeah. And we swapped, like,
recipes and stuff like that.

00:04:18.457 --> 00:04:20.393
And then, yeah,
we cooked it all

00:04:20.393 --> 00:04:23.329
and it was all great
except for the oatmeal.

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:24.696
-Really?
-Yes.

00:04:24.696 --> 00:04:26.098
-Don't take this the wrong way,
that's one of the

00:04:26.098 --> 00:04:27.833
easier things you listed.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:31.971
-No, because after doing
several takes,

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:33.572
oatmeal starts getting thick.

00:04:33.572 --> 00:04:35.508
It's like a plaster.
-Yeah. Yeah, no. Right.

00:04:35.508 --> 00:04:38.578
-After five takes
and I'm, like, trying to do it

00:04:38.578 --> 00:04:40.513
and, like,
make it look seamless.

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:42.915
Sometimes it got stuck.
The spoon got stuck in there.

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:44.817
So that was the only one
that I was like,

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:45.685
"Oh, I should have rethought
that one."

00:04:45.685 --> 00:04:46.853
-So, you would tell actors,

00:04:46.853 --> 00:04:47.753
you should definitely
cook your own food,

00:04:47.753 --> 00:04:48.855
but fake it with the oatmeal.

00:04:48.855 --> 00:04:50.088
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:50.088 --> 00:04:51.657
-Okay, great.
-Yeah, the oatmeal is too much.

00:04:51.657 --> 00:04:53.158
-You also have to smoke
in the movie.

00:04:53.158 --> 00:04:54.894
This did not come
as natural as the...

00:04:54.894 --> 00:04:56.996
-No, no.
I am not a smoker at all.

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:59.565
I'm a vocalist, so no, no, no.

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:03.569
And he -- That was something
when we first talked about it,

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:06.405
he was like,
"Just so you know,

00:05:06.405 --> 00:05:08.407
you're holding it down
with the smoking."

00:05:08.407 --> 00:05:09.508
-Yeah.
-He has a pipe,

00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:10.777
you got the cigs.

00:05:10.777 --> 00:05:12.444
And I was like,
"Oh, well, I don't smoke."

00:05:12.444 --> 00:05:16.949
And he was like, "You have
a package coming in two days."

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:21.287
And he must have like express
shipped two large boxes

00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:24.323
of cartons full of cigarettes.
-Okay.

00:05:24.323 --> 00:05:26.258
-Fake ones.
-Okay, good. Yeah.

00:05:26.258 --> 00:05:28.928
-And as I was doing
the fake ones,

00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:30.396
I was like,
"This isn't cutting it,"

00:05:30.396 --> 00:05:35.234
because it was --
like, the smoke would, like,

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:38.838
plume out like it was a cigar
and it would cover my face up.

00:05:38.838 --> 00:05:40.239
-Yeah.
-And I would still be talking,

00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:42.642
but like a big cloud of smoke
is over my face.

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:44.209
And I was like, "Well,
that's not going to work."

00:05:44.209 --> 00:05:47.779
And so I was nervous that
I was going to get addicted.

00:05:47.779 --> 00:05:49.148
-Yeah.
-So, like, I tried

00:05:49.148 --> 00:05:50.516
different brands and I was like,

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:53.686
"Oh, that might get me addicted.
That one, that one."

00:05:53.686 --> 00:05:56.956
And then it was like, I think
it's called like American Trade.

00:05:56.956 --> 00:05:58.024
-Okay.
-Is that the one?

00:05:58.024 --> 00:05:59.558
Or American Spirit.
-Yes, yes, yes.

00:05:59.558 --> 00:06:02.695
-Yes. The blue one.
The blue one.

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:06.832
That was like the perfect dosage
where I was like, "Okay,

00:06:06.832 --> 00:06:08.501
I can do this.

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:10.770
I'm not going to get addicted,
and it's real

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:11.970
so it's going to look real."

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:15.675
And so I would ask people

00:06:15.675 --> 00:06:17.909
who smoked like,
"Does this look right?"

00:06:17.909 --> 00:06:19.344
I would watch old movies.

00:06:19.344 --> 00:06:20.913
-Oh that's good.
Who's the best old movie...?

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:22.114
-Bette Davis.
-Bette Davis.

00:06:22.114 --> 00:06:24.116
-Bette Davis, Bette Davis.
-Great.

00:06:24.116 --> 00:06:25.785
She has it on lock.

00:06:25.785 --> 00:06:28.187
So I would, like,
constantly have a cigarette,

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:31.791
because it's a choreography
of knowing when to pull it,

00:06:31.791 --> 00:06:34.827
to drag it,
and then do you hold it?

00:06:34.827 --> 00:06:37.229
Can we start talking now?
Do I release it now?

00:06:37.229 --> 00:06:38.397
Am I going to do,
like, the weed

00:06:38.397 --> 00:06:40.299
like [raspy muttering]
like that?

00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:43.336
No, it's the '70s
and that wouldn't make sense.

00:06:43.336 --> 00:06:46.472
So I was like, "I got to figure
this out,"

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:48.474
and like, you know,
to try to track, like,

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:49.909
okay, well,
she's upset right now,

00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:52.544
so she's going through
these cigs faster

00:06:52.544 --> 00:06:53.913
than in other situations.

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:55.681
So it was
a really cool, fun thing

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:57.416
to just have --
-That's amazing.

00:06:57.416 --> 00:06:58.550
-Yeah, that and dialect.

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:01.487
It was just like
great anchors for me.

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:03.088
-It's fantastic because
I will say it is

00:07:03.088 --> 00:07:06.592
the most naturally acted movie
by all of you.

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:08.394
-Thank you.
-And now I'm going to go back

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:09.962
and watch it and realize like,
"Oh, my God,

00:07:09.962 --> 00:07:11.897
all she's thinking about
is oatmeal and cigarettes."

00:07:11.897 --> 00:07:13.866
-Yes! Exactly!
-That's literally all --

00:07:13.866 --> 00:07:15.701
She's like,
"Smoke, stir, smoke, stir."

00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:17.903
-Smoke, smoke, smoke, smoke.
-Aah!

00:07:17.903 --> 00:07:21.107
It's a wonderful film.
You're receiving Oscar buzz,

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:23.342
knock on wood,
but deservedly so.

00:07:23.342 --> 00:07:24.276
-Thank you.
-Thanks for being here.

00:07:24.276 --> 00:07:25.278
-Thank you.
-You guys,

00:07:25.278 --> 00:07:27.213
That's Da'Vine Joy Randolph.

00:07:27.213 --> 00:07:28.514
"The Holdovers" in theaters now.

00:07:28.514 --> 00:07:29.715
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

